    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-u/q1",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q1-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The events A and B satisfy P ( ) A = 0.4 ,          ( P A B = ) 0.6 ,        ( P A ∪ B ) = 2P ( A ∩ B ) . Find the value of ( P B A′ . ) ( ) 9\" style=\"--madas-width:590.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q1-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Let b = P(B). [B1] [A1] The conditional probability gives P(A ∩B) = 0.6b. [A1] By inclusion–exclusion the union is 0.4 + b −0.6b = 0.4 + 0.4b, [A1] which must equal 1.2b. [A1] Thus b = 0.5 [A1] and the intersection is 0.3. [A1] The alternative source diagram labels A-only, both, B-only and neither by x, y, z, w. Writing out the conditions in that notation gives x + y = 0.4, y/(y + z) = 0.6, x + y + z = 2y, x + y + z + w = 1. The second relation gives 0.4y = 0.6z, hence y = 1.5z.\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q1-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Now x + 1.5z = 0.4, and the third equation gives x −0.5z = 0. Subtract to obtain 2z = 0.4, so z = 0.2, x = 0.1, y = 0.3 and w = 1 −0.1 −0.3 −0.2 . = 0.4 The solved probabilities give y = 0.3, x = 0.4 −y = 0.1, z = 0.5 −y = 0.2, w = 1 −x −y −z = 0.4. On restricting to A′ the ratio is z/(z + w). Therefore P(B | A′) = 0.5 −0.3 1 −0.4 [M1] [A1] = 1 3 .\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 1 is 9 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The events A and B satisfy P ( ) A = 0.4 ,          ( P A B = ) 0.6 ,        ( P A ∪ B ) = 2P ( A ∩ B ) . Find the value of ( P B A′ . ) ( ) 9"
  },
  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-u/q2",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q2-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The probability distribution of a discrete random variable X is given by  2 P  ( X = r ) r = 1,2,3,4,5,... =  3 P ( X = r + 1 )  0 otherwise ( ) 6 Determine ( P 2 ≤ X ≤ 4 ) .\" style=\"--madas-width:586.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q2-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"If p1 = P(X = 1), [B1] the probabilities are p1(2/3)r−1 for r ≥1. [A1] Their sum is p1/(1 −2/3) = 1, [A1] giving p1 = 1/3. [A1] In order the first four masses are 1/3, 2/9, 4/27, 8/81. On a denominator of 81, the last three contribute 18 + 12 + 8 = 38. Hence P(2 ≤X ≤4) = 1 2 3 + 4 9 + 8  = 38 81 . [M1] [A1] 3 27\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 2 is 6 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The probability distribution of a discrete random variable X is given by  2 P  ( X = r ) r = 1,2,3,4,5,... =  3 P ( X = r + 1 )  0 otherwise ( ) 6 Determine ( P 2 ≤ X ≤ 4 ) ."
  },
  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-u/q3",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q3-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The number of miles Mark’s motorbike can travel on a full tank of petrol, can be modelled by a Normal distribution with a mean of 135 and a standard deviation of 12. a) Determine the probability that Mark can travel at least 165 miles on a full tank of petrol. ( ) 4 b) Find, to the nearest mile, the longest journey that Mark can make, if he is to have at least a 90% chance of completing it on a single full tank of petrol. ( ) 4 c) Given that Mark has a 90% chance of completing a journey on a single full tank of petrol find the probability that this journey will not exceed 165 miles. ( ) 4\" style=\"--madas-width:594.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q3-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Worked solution diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:554.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q3-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(a) Standardising 165 gives (165 −135)/12 = 2.5. [M1] [A1] Thus P(X ≥165) = 1 −Φ(2.5) ≈0.006210 . [M1] [A1] (b) The exact threshold is d = 135 + 12Φ−1(0.10) ≈119.621 miles. [B1] Reported to the nearest mile as the question requests, this threshold is 120 miles . If planning a journey of a whole number of miles, round down to maintain the guarantee, as in the scan: The greatest whole-mile length meeting the probability requirement is 119 miles . [M1] [A1] At 120 miles the probability would be below 0.9. [A1] (c) With d exact, P(X ≥d) = 0.90 [B1] and {X &gt; 165} ⊆{X ≥d}. [A1] Standardising d gives\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q3-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"(d −135)/12 = −1.28155 . . . . The joint interval from d through 165 has probability 0.9 −0.0062097 . . .; divide this by 0.9. The conditional probability is 1 −[1 −Φ(2.5)]/0.90 ≈0.9931 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 3 is 12 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The number of miles Mark’s motorbike can travel on a full tank of petrol, can be modelled by a Normal distribution with a mean of 135 and a standard deviation of 12. a) Determine the probability that Mark can travel at least 165 miles on a full tank of petrol. ( ) 4 b) Find, to the nearest mile, the longest journey that Mark can make, if he is to have at least a 90% chance of completing it on a single full tank of petrol. ( ) 4 c) Given that Mark has a 90% chance of completing a journey on a single full tank of petrol find the probability that this journey will not exceed 165 miles. ( ) 4"
  },
