    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-u/q8",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q8-question-1.svg\" alt=\"During hot days, an ice cream van sells a large number of ice cream cones containing either  1, 2 or 3 scoops of ice cream. The respective probabilities of a customer buying a 1, 2 or 3 scoop ice cream cone are 1 6 , 1 2 or 1 3 . A random sample of n customers is examined, each customer having bought an ice cream cone from this van. The probability that more than n scoops of ice cream are ordered by these n customers is greater than 0.9999 . Determine the smallest possible value of n . ( ) 8\" style=\"--madas-width:577.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q8-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Every order has at least one scoop, so the only way to have total n is for every customer to order exactly one scoop: [B1] [A1] The required inequality is 1 −(1/6)n &gt; 0.9999, [M1] [A1] or (1/6)n &lt; 10−4. [M1] [A1] Taking logarithms gives n ln(1/6) &lt; −4 ln 10, so n &gt; 4 ln 10/ ln 6 = 5.140 . . . . Since 65 = 7776 &lt; 104 &lt; 66 = 46656, the least integer is 6 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 8 is 8 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "During hot days, an ice cream van sells a large number of ice cream cones containing either  1, 2 or 3 scoops of ice cream. The respective probabilities of a customer buying a 1, 2 or 3 scoop ice cream cone are 1 6 , 1 2 or 1 3 . A random sample of n customers is examined, each customer having bought an ice cream cone from this van. The probability that more than n scoops of ice cream are ordered by these n customers is greater than 0.9999 . Determine the smallest possible value of n . ( ) 8"
