    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-u/q10",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q10-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The motions of two cars, A and B , is observed as they travel between two sets of traffic lights, on a straight road, which are 600 m apart. At time t = 0 , A passes through the first set of lights, accelerating uniformly from a ms− to a speed 20 1 ms−. 1 speed of 4 As soon as this speed is reached it begins to decelerate uniformly for 30 s , coming to rest at the next set of traffic lights. ms−. 1 At time t = 0 , B passes through the first set of lights, with constant speed of 12 It maintains this speed until it begins to decelerate uniformly, coming to rest at the second set of lights at the same time as A .\" style=\"--madas-width:564.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q10-question-2.svg\" alt=\"a) Sketch, on the same speed time graph the motions of A and B . ( ) 4 b) Find the deceleration of B , just as it arrives at the second set of lights. ( ) 8\" style=\"--madas-width:576.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q10-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) Draw both motions on common speed and time axes. For A, start at 4 m s−1 and draw a straight rising segment to 20 m s−1. [M1] Draw the straight braking segment to rest 30 seconds later: [B1] For B, draw the horizontal segment at 12 m s−1, [B1] then a straight braking segment reaching zero at the same finishing time as A. [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:674.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q10-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(b) Let x be the time for which A accelerates and T the time for which B brakes: The areas under the graphs are each 600 m. For A, the trapezium contributes 1 2(4 + 20)x [M1] and the triangle contributes 1 2(30)(20) = 300. [A1] Thus 12x + 300 = 600 gives x = 25. [A1] Both motions last 25 + 30 = 55 seconds: [A1] For B, add the rectangle and the triangle: 12(55 −T) + 1 2(12)T = 600. [A1] This gives 660−6T = 600, hence T = 10. [A1] Equivalently take the entire area for B as the trapezium 1 2[55 + (55 −T)]12 = 600. Divide by 6, giving 110 −T = 100.\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-u/q10-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"The deceleration is the magnitude of the braking gradient, ∆v/T, [A1] so it equals 12/10 = 1.2 m s−2 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 10 is 12 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The motions of two cars, A and B , is observed as they travel between two sets of traffic lights, on a straight road, which are 600 m apart. At time t = 0 , A passes through the first set of lights, accelerating uniformly from a ms− to a speed 20 1 ms−. 1 speed of 4 As soon as this speed is reached it begins to decelerate uniformly for 30 s , coming to rest at the next set of traffic lights. ms−. 1 At time t = 0 , B passes through the first set of lights, with constant speed of 12 It maintains this speed until it begins to decelerate uniformly, coming to rest at the second set of lights at the same time as A . a) Sketch, on the same speed time graph the motions of A and B . ( ) 4 b) Find the deceleration of B , just as it arrives at the second set of lights. ( ) 8"
